293 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1518 0 R
/E (This project presents the design and development of a Digital Twin-based Robotic Arm System for real-time monitoring, simulation, and control. The system consists of a 5-Degree-of-Freedom \(5-DOF\) robotic arm fabricated using 3D-printed components and actuated by SG90 servo motors. A virtual replica of the robotic arm is created in Unity, enabling real-time visualization and interaction with the physical system.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
294 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1519 0 R
/E (The physical robotic arm is controlled using a microcontroller-based architecture integrated with wireless communication for remote operation. To improve the intelligence of the system, a machine learning model is developed using a dataset generated from the robotic arm's forward kinematics. The model predicts joint angles based on the desired end-effector position, enabling efficient inverse kinematic estimation. The generated dataset includes various combinations of joint angles and corresponding Cartesian coordinates of the robotic arm.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
295 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1520 0 R
/E (The Digital Twin framework establishes synchronization between the physical and virtual environments, allowing users to observe arm movements, validate control strategies, and analyze system performance in a risk-free virtual space. The project demonstrates the potential of Digital Twin technology in robotics, industrial training, and automation applications by combining physical hardware, simulation, and artificial intelligence techniques.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
296 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1521 0 R
/E (The developed system provides a cost-effective platform for robotic experimentation, remote monitoring, operator training, and future research in smart manufacturing and cyber-physical systems.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
