365 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 236 0 R
/E (The mechanical design of the robotic arm was developed to achieve flexibility, stability, and ease of control while maintaining a lightweight structure. A 5-Degree-of-Freedom \(5-DOF\) configuration was selected to provide sufficient mobility for performing object manipulation and positioning tasks. The five joints enable the arm to perform base rotation, shoulder movement, elbow movement, wrist movement, and gripper operation.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
366 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1568 0 R
/E (Joint arrangement)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
367 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 236 0 R
/E (The joints are arranged in a serial manipulator configuration, where each joint contributes to the overall motion of the end effector. SG90 servo motors are used at each joint to provide rotational motion and precise angular control. This arrangement allows the robotic arm to reach different positions within its workspace while maintaining a simple and cost-effective design.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
368 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1569 0 R
/E (Link lengths)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
369 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 236 0 R
/E (The robotic arm consists of four main links with lengths of L1 = 2 cm, L2 = 6.2 cm, L3 = 6.3 cm, and L4 = 6.7 cm. These link dimensions were selected to provide an adequate working range while ensuring structural stability and minimizing the load on the servo motors. The combination of link lengths and joint configuration enables the arm to achieve smooth movement and effective reach for pick-and-place operations and experimental tasks.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
