410 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1586 0 R
/E (The fabricated parts of the robotic arm were manufactured using a 3D printer based on the CAD models designed for the project. PLA \(Polylactic Acid\) filament was selected as the printing material due to its lightweight nature, ease of printing, dimensional stability, and sufficient mechanical strength for prototype development. Each component was printed individually and later assembled to form the complete 5-DOF robotic arm.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
411 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1587 0 R
/E (The fabricated components include the base platform, base support frame, servo motor mounts, shoulder link, elbow link, wrist link, gripper mechanism, gripper fingers, gear wheels, and connecting brackets. These parts were designed with appropriate mounting holes to accommodate SG90 servo motors and fastening screws, ensuring accurate alignment and smooth rotational movement of each joint.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
412 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1588 0 R
/E (After fabrication, all parts were carefully inspected for dimensional accuracy, surface quality, and proper fit. Minor finishing operations, such as removing support material and smoothing the edges, were carried out before assembly. The fabricated components were then assembled using screws and servo motors to obtain a rigid and lightweight robotic arm capable of performing controlled pick-and-place operations.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
413 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1589 0 R
/E (The use of 3D-printed fabricated parts significantly reduced manufacturing cost and development time while allowing easy modification and replacement of individual components. This approach also provided flexibility for future design improvements and supported the rapid prototyping requirements of the digital twin-based robotic arm system.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
